          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "A useful critique starts by restating what the project is trying to accomplish. Imagine a small community learning center on a sloped urban site. Its intent is to create an accessible public threshold, flexible classrooms, daylight, and a visible connection to the neighborhood. Before evaluating form, the reviewer names the users, program, site conditions, and performance goals. This gives every later comment a reason. Without intent, critique collapses into personal preference; with intent, each observation can test whether the proposal supports the project's stated aims."
