        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Architecture becomes credible when systems reinforce the spatial idea. A roof that brings daylight may also carry structure, drainage, insulation, and mechanical distribution. Moving one edge can alter energy performance, acoustics, cost, and the experience of the room. An integrative review therefore asks what each change improves and what it affects elsewhere. Layered overlays make dependencies visible. The goal is not maximum complexity; it is coordination, so environmental and technical performance strengthen rather than undermine the intended experience."
